<file path="experiments/analysis-efficiency/model-context.md">
# Observe the client boundary before claiming token savings

Continues [#2016](https://github.com/morluto/rea/issues/2016) after the managed
summary [PR #2413](https://github.com/morluto/rea/pull/2413) merged as
`82cfcee19f18b0eeae9a330ba04e0411cdf85225`. That opt-in view has verified smaller
delivery and complete-default migration, while its original real-model cohort
does not establish token savings. This follow-up measures why raw reply bytes
and aggregate model usage cannot be treated as interchangeable observations.

## Concrete diagnostic observation

An individual real Codex CLI 0.161.0 control uses frozen runtime
`ae8051302b5834322fa00cfa31c968ae74ab6ed9`, the unchanged ShareX identity question,
requested `gpt-5.6-terra` high and the same two enabled REA tools. A private
diagnostic copy removes the original runner's explicit `--ephemeral` flag.
Authentication/profile cleanup still runs; only selected tool/usage items are
retained after filtering the owned rollout. Its source rollout SHA-256 is
`d4df41d6187880d177f00b7533d85ad032c9c4ec502a39e7d5a566180e10461b` and the retained
filtered-item SHA-256 is
`0d5b4fb67c5674d1ba14ac32ae37e9a72b0368f4d9f5f9f81eb4d13193cda260`.
The original whole-copy is removed; account metadata, world state, messages and
internal passthrough are not retained in the filtered receipt.

Despite the requested `direct` configuration label, the observed client uses
three JavaScript `exec` calls. First it filters `ALL_TOOLS` with a broad
`/REA|reverse|artifact|managed|assembly|metadata/i` expression and prints matching
metadata. The client reports truncation of a 164,005-token original output.
This is the client's output measurement, not billed input or a complete inventory.
Next it invokes complete managed inspection but selects requested normalized
identity/classification fields before printing. Finally it invokes native-boundary
inspection and prints selected fields. The actual original complete MCP replies
remain available in the separate public-event transcript.

The persisted custom-tool output values are 41,704 / 2,154 / 13,489 UTF-8 bytes.
These are the serialized recorded output **values**, not raw wire bytes or exact
submitted model requests. The trace reports four per-response usage observations:

| Response |  Input | Cached input | Output |
| -------- | -----: | -----------: | -----: |
| 1        | 10,400 |            0 |    132 |
| 2        | 19,992 |        9,984 |    456 |
| 3        | 21,242 |       19,200 |    251 |
| 4        | 24,799 |       20,224 |  1,535 |

Their totals match public turn usage: 76,433 input, 49,408 cached, 27,025 uncached
and 2,374 output, with 61.436 seconds observed wall time. Two MCP calls alone do
not describe the client's discovery/execution rounds. This proves the observed
control can project a complete result before printing it; it does not establish
that every earlier control/candidate did the same, or attribute every token
difference to discovery. The earlier `direct` label specifies requested client
configuration, not a verified absence of an executor.

Initial transcript-capture attempts retained empty source lists because the
original runner intentionally passed `--ephemeral`. A first private flag-removal
edit missed its newline and failed; that candidate remains an unsuccessful
capture attempt. The later corrected control is an individual diagnostic,
not a comparable pair with that candidate. Other exploratory aggregates remain
private and are not silently folded into the original four-trial cohort.

## Reproducible optional capture

`agent-trial.py --capture-tool-context` omits `--ephemeral` only for that explicitly
selected temporary trial. Its default is unchanged. The profile still disappears
in `finally`, including when capture fails. The capture reads only its owned
`sessions/*.jsonl` records, rejects symlink sources, and retains:

- Custom/function tool calls and replies, preserving call IDs and exact values.
- Per-response token counts and cumulative counts, with original response IDs.
- Client token-count events, retaining explicit unknown `info: null`.

It excludes session/account metadata, world state, user/developer messages,
encrypted reasoning, internal passthrough and unknown nested usage fields. It
does not copy authentication, configuration or the ambient environment. Receipts
record source/retained digests, event counts, malformed-line failures and missing
rollouts as unavailable. Capture is an observation of persisted client items,
not proof of the exact request accepted by a remote model service.

Run the same frozen runtime, question, tool selection and capture mode on both
sides. Preserve source/settings hashes and separate requested versus observed
behavior. Use the existing resource wrapper with unchanged one CPU, 4 GiB hard
memory, 3 GiB early-stop, no swap and task/host safeguards. Final physical counters
remain unknown when transient cgroups disappear; this instrumentation does not
repair that measurement limitation.

The focused Python tests run through the existing CI `test:python` gate.
They cover account/ambient exclusion while preserving caller data, malformed and
missing records, foreign symlinks, and capture failure without copied-credential
leakage. The cleanup test simulates a CLI and establishes only cleanup semantics;
it is not real-model/provider evidence.

## An explicit discovery-guidance experiment

`--guide-enabled-tools` is another opt-in experiment flag. It requires a nonempty
`--enabled-tools` list and adds the exact corresponding `mcp__rea__...` function
names to the temporary developer instructions, requesting only exact-name
metadata discovery. It preserves the analyst question, operation order, tool
selection and provider inputs. The default instruction is unchanged. Missing or
invalid selected tools fail before creating an output/profile. The test gate
also verifies the new prerequisite without acquiring a profile.

Compare guided and unguided runs on the **same** frozen REA runtime with matching
capture mode and requested facts. A smaller discovery response or additional
hint is not itself a task/token success: grade answers, count all response usage,
preserve failed/truncated runs and repeat both orders before recommending this
guidance. This is a caller-side experiment, not a new REA tool or provider preset.

## First committed capture pair

Checkpoint `9ebdcc458c399b9bdecaaca2ef47422888231181` runs the unchanged question
against frozen control `ae8051302b5834322fa00cfa31c968ae74ab6ed9` and candidate
`8bc1601ca51e417ca472d90ea0367e9418a1a0f2`, both with capture enabled and no tool
guidance. Both captures succeed with 10 retained events, unchanged source bytes,
removed profiles, two successful MCP calls and passing selected factual checks.
Full grader gates remain false because final physical resource counters are
unavailable; factual subsets do not override that limitation.

| Side              |  Input | Cached | Uncached | Output | Wall seconds |
| ----------------- | -----: | -----: | -------: | -----: | -----------: |
| Complete control  | 52,656 | 27,136 |   25,520 |  2,015 |       57.736 |
| Summary candidate | 56,655 | 29,184 |   27,471 |  1,534 |       55.055 |

Both clients make two executor calls and three response-usage observations.
First they print broad discovered metadata, with truncation. Then each executor
sequentially performs both MCP operations, preserving selected-target dependency
order. The control prints a 2,086-byte output value; the candidate prints 22,770
bytes. Their discovery output values are 41,723 and 41,675 bytes. These are
recorded serialized values and remain distinct from raw MCP envelopes, billed
tokens and exact submitted requests. Both requested modes are `direct`; the
actual executor use is separately observed.

The candidate's smaller raw metadata reply does not result in a smaller printed
client value in this pair. Candidate input/uncached tokens remain higher. The
observed projection and round counts are useful constraints for the next
experiment; this one pair does not attribute all usage differences to either
formatting or discovery, or establish a reliable speedup.

## Same-runtime guided discovery, repeated in both orders

Checkpoint `e20bd0e2a905f06bc8c56fe04b0e61f3da88636a` runs four actual Codex trials
in unguided/guided/guided/unguided order. All use the **same** frozen candidate
runtime, artifact, original question, requested model/reasoning, two selected
tools, provider settings and capture mode. Only the explicit discovery-guidance
treatment changes. Every trial selects artifact summary and makes the same two
successful MCP calls in dependency order; source bytes stay unchanged, captures
succeed and profiles are removed.

| Trial      |   Input | Cached | Uncached | Output | Seconds | Observed responses |
| ---------- | ------: | -----: | -------: | -----: | ------: | -----------------: |
| Unguided 1 |  77,251 | 50,432 |   26,819 |  1,931 |  62.066 |                  4 |
| Guided 1   |  39,175 | 23,040 |   16,135 |  1,763 |  55.658 |                  3 |
| Guided 2   |  58,336 | 38,144 |   20,192 |  1,626 |  50.685 |                  4 |
| Unguided 2 | 103,301 | 72,704 |   30,597 |  1,983 |  60.336 |                  5 |

Both guided discovery output values repeat at 12,336 bytes without truncation,
versus 41,709 bytes with truncation on both unguided trials. The recorded
executor/output usage explains observable discovery and round differences; it
does not certify exact remote request contents or attribute every token to the
hint. CPU samples are 6.303–6.554 seconds and peak family memory 684,519,424–
712,028,160 bytes, with no guard abort. Final resource counters remain unknown.
These samples do not establish a memory or CPU improvement.

Unguided 1 omits `classification.container` and fails the selected factual gate;
it is not a fully acceptable comparison answer. Guided 1 and Unguided 2 pass
that gate. Guided 2 initially fails exact coverage-object equality because it
adds `module_ref_count: 0` and `pinvoke_import_count: 0` to the explicitly named
native scope. Both numbers exactly match the producer's returned arrays. The
grader now explicitly verifies only these two known additional counts, including
integer type, while still requiring all original coverage facts. Unknown extra
claims, conflicting aliases, wrong counts, boolean/string counts and omitted
issues continue to fail. Source coverage remains unchanged; returned-row counts
do not establish global absence. Original failure receipts remain preserved;
the regrade separately records its new source hash. Guided 2 then passes selected
facts, but every **full** gate still fails unknown final physical resource checks.

The second comparison has two factually acceptable answers: observed guided input
falls about 43.5%, uncached input about 34.0%, and wall time about 16.0%. The first
comparison has an incomplete unguided answer and is reported without counting it
as equivalent completed work. Two trials per treatment on one question/application
remain a preliminary signal, not a general default recommendation. The hint stays
opt-in; wider questions/domains, count/coverage representation and client round
behavior need further verification before promoting it into general guidance.

## Real Android follow-up: the primary outcomes are mixed

Checkpoint `22834c7d73056e2480b426848f58c31af0c307bc` checks the pinned real
NewPipe 0.29.1 APK using actual configured JADX 0.7.1/Java, the original Android
question and the same frozen candidate runtime. First a fresh SDK workflow
validates three live advertised operations and produces the comparison facts:
package/version identity, every case-sensitive MainActivity match and all
reported onCreate entries, preserving signatures, overload indices and null DEX
descriptors. It takes 26.961 seconds; the owned scope samples 1,234,735,104 bytes.

Both subsequent actual Codex trials use the same three selected tool names,
capture mode, model/reasoning, question and provider settings. Only discovery
guidance changes. Each makes three successful MCP calls and five observed model
responses, passes selected facts against the fresh actual producer, preserves
source bytes and removes its temporary profile. No APK execution is requested.

| Trial    |  Input | Cached | Uncached | Output | Wall seconds | Sampled peak bytes |
| -------- | -----: | -----: | -------: | -----: | -----------: | -----------------: |
| Unguided | 99,913 | 80,640 |   19,273 |  1,527 |       66.797 |      1,405,829,120 |
| Guided   | 89,220 | 62,464 |   26,756 |  2,142 |       76.936 |      1,345,576,960 |

Total input falls about 10.7%, but uncached input increases and wall time is
longer. One pair does not demonstrate improved Android performance or memory.
No guard abort occurs; final counters remain unknown and full resource gates
remain false. This second domain is a concrete counterexample to generalizing
the favorable managed comparison. Keep both flags optional and continue
task-specific experiments before changing installed/default agent guidance.
</file>

<file path="experiments/analysis-efficiency/native-entry-views.md">
# Native PE and DOS entry-view checkpoint

Continuation of [#2016](https://github.com/morluto/rea/issues/2016) after
#2122 and #2193 merged. Real Ghidra observations and real Codex CLI answers
support the selected native facets on these shipped inputs. They do not justify
an automatic native summary policy: the four selected replies are larger than
the complete dossier for the small PE entry, and smaller for the DOS entry.
The initial complete analysis still runs and is delivered in both cases.

This checkpoint changes experiment scripts, corpus inputs and grading only.
Existing production CLI/MCP contracts, defaults and session behavior are unchanged.

## Inputs and runtime

Production runtime: main `10400fdb57e4226c5ce15af17968a664f35498ce`,
including the merged WASM summary work and subsequent main changes. Node
24.18.0/npm 11.16.0, locked dependencies, actual Ghidra 12.1.4 on Linux.
Default auto-analysis remains enabled, with a 768 MiB JVM ceiling and one
active processor. The whole process family uses the existing one-CPU,
4 GiB hard/3 GiB early-stop, no-swap, bounded-task resource scope.

| Shipped artifact                   | Selected bytes | SHA-256                                                            | Static scope                                                                                                  |
| ---------------------------------- | -------------: | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| ShareX 19.0.2 `ShareX.exe`         |        198,656 | `46af1ccc514b395cb703633abb5a505a771942b7db9b1915f31aeea541b3685f` | Native PE32+ x86-64 component with no CLR directory; entry `0x1400125c0` derived from original image base/RVA |
| FreeDOS EDLIN 2.24 `BIN/EDLIN.EXE` |         31,492 | `96b2b6d543e2d8bdc83e313756444e103339eb5ee2c1229abe3607539b31ad6e` | Original UPX-compressed DOS MZ; admitted entry `0x10000`, independently verified static load image            |

Acquisition URLs, archive sizes/digests and exact regular ZIP members are in
[corpus.json](corpus.json). EDLIN's package `APPINFO/EDLIN.LSM` reports its
version and UPX compression. No binary was executed, unpacked or committed.
The independent PE header does not establish Windows runtime behavior.
DOS load-image verification and unpacking-stub pseudocode do not recover the
editor's application logic or establish DOS execution. DOS COM is still untested.

The SDK and frozen model runtime share the exact `dist/` and `bridge/` file
table digest:
`e2b67eb0966072fd8b159edfab074cbaf1db9bfc0ac9c57d25ba1435cc0fdbcf`.
The model copy was independently checked against all 1,375 compiled files and
30 bridge files. Runtime copies and detailed receipts remain outside Git.

## Public SDK observations

The workflow opens the selected target with Ghidra, resolves the entry,
calls `analyze_function` once, reads retained procedure/pseudocode/callers/callees
facets, and closes the ephemeral session. DOS obtains its address from
`inspect_native_load_image` before resolving the procedure. Each facet is checked
against the complete producer facts, original parent ID, target digest and distinct
derived Evidence ID. The actual SDK validates named advertised output contracts.

| Entry        | Complete dossier JSON bytes | Four facet replies combined | Workflow seconds | First provider query seconds | Last-observed family CPU seconds | Sampled family peak MiB |
| ------------ | --------------------------: | --------------------------: | ---------------: | ---------------------------: | -------------------------------: | ----------------------: |
| ShareX PE    |                      50,936 |                      68,257 |           63.123 |                       57.490 |                           63.660 |                1,197.85 |
| EDLIN DOS MZ |                     224,670 |                      70,539 |           26.436 |                       21.366 |                           26.899 |                  939.25 |

These are exploratory observations, not a paired implementation performance
cohort. JSON byte counts include the SDK tool-result representations, excluding
catalog discovery and wire framing; they are not model token counts. Four PE
facets cost about 34% more bytes than that dossier. The DOS facets cost about
69% fewer bytes **when reading already-retained Evidence**. Their first analysis
still returned the full dossier; summing both paths increases first-task traffic.
The first provider query includes the observable wait for the imported Program
and its analysis, not an independently measured engine-only stage.

The observed PE entry contains 18 analyzed bytes, zero recorded callers and two
resolved callees. Ghidra's terminal-call caveat remains explicit. The DOS entry
contains 53 analyzed bytes and zero recorded callers/callees; overlapping
instruction and abnormal stack-frame warnings remain in the pseudocode.
Neither zero count establishes absence of unresolved runtime calls.

An earlier PE probe completed production analysis and two facets, then failed
its harness assertion: a pseudocode page is `{text, unit}`, not a string. That
failed attempt remains archived and is excluded from successful workflow figures.
The corrected exploratory SDK driver fingerprints are
`4876c0ecaa84069ddc508e29649715c6c914bd3c4960a76fa9724b5992123699`
and native-helper `76023ad45cd1edab57dcd4a8ccf29a9b8a8378029ff2d9711ae35c73940e80d6`.
The published helper in `6d7f6885c` removes redundant scopes; replaying the actual
PE/DOS replies reproduces every answer field and the same 9/10 calls. Recorded
real jq entry and entry/main replies also preserve their selected facts, profile
and 4/12-call ordering. These replays are driver regression checks, not fresh
provider runs or timing measurements.

## Real Codex CLI observations

Three serial runs used Codex CLI 0.161.0, requested alias `gpt-5.6-terra`, high
reasoning, temporary isolated profiles and the frozen modified REA MCP runtime.
Resolved server-side model identity is not inferred. The same explicit seven-tool
subset was enabled: open, containing-procedure resolution, dossier, analysis view,
load image, retained Evidence discovery and close. This is not full-catalog tool
selection verification. Questions are [PE](questions/native-pe.txt) and
[DOS](questions/native-dos.txt).

| Trial | Total input | Cached input | Uncached input | Output | Seconds | Calls / failed |
| ----- | ----------: | -----------: | -------------: | -----: | ------: | -------------: |
| PE 1  |     451,337 |      399,616 |         51,721 |  3,820 | 130.073 |         12 / 2 |
| DOS 1 |     730,578 |      658,944 |         71,634 |  3,310 | 134.435 |          9 / 0 |
| PE 2  |     498,322 |      429,056 |         69,266 |  3,038 | 192.373 |          9 / 1 |

All three answers match the selected original digest, address, procedure
classification/signature/locals, inclusive body ranges, body byte count,
pseudocode prefix and relationship counts. Actual Evidence references were
observed in the responses; target bytes remained unchanged, close completed and
the copied profiles were removed. DOS additionally matches the verified load
status and every supplied load-image metadata field. Manual review preserves
static/packed-target and terminal-flow qualifications.

The first grader expected only particular nested JSON shapes. It rejected PE's
equivalent text-page object/inclusive-range label and could not grade DOS's
explicit `entry @ 0x10000` display. The updated grader compares exact source
values for those representations without parsing displays into lookup identities.
It still rejects wrong text units, returned lengths, body endpoints and missing
facts. Original answers and first grading attempts remain archived; regrading
does not repeat model calls or change their tokens/times.

The PE agent hit the advertised-but-unimplemented native summary recipe: both
trials attempted `view: {kind: "summary"}`; the first also tried the invalid
native facet `summary`. Those failed calls and all subsequent recovery/repeated
reads remain included. The public SDK independently reproduced `invalid_request`
for native summary on both inputs. This pre-existing contract-description mismatch
is recorded in `/tmp/rea-analysis-efficiency-bugs-20261012.md` for team repair,
as requested; this branch does not repair it.

Model sampled peaks were 1,318.74 / 1,017.70 / 1,234.92 MiB, with last-observed
family CPU 72.956 / 71.534 / 74.089 seconds. SDK/model scopes did not abort and
their last-observed limit-event counters were zero. Final cgroup counters remained
unavailable after scope teardown: the strict resource-qualified overall grades
remain unverified. The selected fact/lifecycle checks pass; no universal latency,
OOM absence or CPU/memory saving is inferred from these samples.

## Reproduction and next decision

Use the pinned toolchain and build the selected production revision. Keep its
runtime copy, matching dependencies, provider settings, binaries and receipts
outside the checkout, as described in [README.md](README.md).

```sh
python3 experiments/analysis-efficiency/resource-run.py -- \
  node experiments/analysis-efficiency/measure.mjs \
  --runtime-root /absolute/frozen-runtime --revision EXACT_PRODUCTION_SHA \
  --artifact sharex.exe --path /absolute/ShareX.exe \
  --providers /absolute/explicit-providers.json \
  --native-question entry-views --schemas selected \
  --output /absolute/new-native-measurement
```

For DOS select `--artifact freedos.edlin.exe` and the original extracted
`EDLIN.EXE`. `--native-question entry` retains the existing compact experimental
answer; `entry-views` performs additional verification reads and the explicit
summary refusal probe. It does not select summary delivery from `analyze_function`.
The helper fingerprint participates in comparison, and missing native helper
fingerprints are not treated as a comparable current driver.

The reviewed `entry-views` answer separates selected facet facts from run-specific
references: `evidence_ids.retained_views` retains each facet's original view and
parent ID, while `retained_views` holds its item, coverage, limitations and unknowns.
DOS load-image facts retain their source ID and must match the selected carrier
digest before their entry address can be used; a stale result closes the session
without querying a procedure.
Derived view IDs are checked against every prior producer reference as well as
other derived views. DOS model grades require the actual completed load-image
response and its reported source ID; status, digest, entry and supplied metadata
are checked against that observation. Missing, failed or unreferenced responses
cannot establish load-image verification.
The comparator preserves both runs' reference maps and compares the facts. Existing
`entry`/`entry-and-main` answers keep their ID arrays. Historical SDK receipts above
retain their original pre-review representation; no replies or measurements were
rewritten, and no provider/model runs were repeated for this representation fix.

Relevant validation includes the real workflows above, recorded-reply regression,
grader negative probes, JavaScript/Python syntax, formatting/diff checks and
`npm run check:fast`. The cold type/lint gate passed in 112.602 seconds with
sampled peak 3,220,914,176 bytes and five last-observed `memory.high` events;
this gate is not a performance-qualified experiment. Unrelated domain suites,
packaging and earlier model cohorts were not rerun for this experiment-only change.

The next useful comparison is question-specific native primitives versus a
complete dossier, with identical requested facts and actual model trials.
Larger PE functions and another unpacked DOS/COM application can extend the
coverage progressively. Preserve existing complete defaults and solve the
summary-description bug separately before recommending a native summary route.
</file>

<file path="experiments/analysis-efficiency/native-entry.mjs">
import { open, readFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { isDeepStrictEqual } from "node:util";
export const NATIVE_ARTIFACTS = ["jq", "sharex.exe", "freedos.edlin.exe"];
const normalized = (value) => value.normalized_result;
const retainedEntryViews = async (
  call,
  info,
  inputSha,
  ToolFailure,
  sourceEvidenceIds,
) => {
  const n = normalized(info);
  const retainedViews = [];
  const retainedViewEvidence = [];
  const observedEvidenceIds = new Set([info.evidence_id, ...sourceEvidenceIds]);
  let nativeSummaryOutcome, summaryEvidenceId;
  for (const facet of ["procedure", "pseudocode", "callers", "callees"]) {
    const selected = await call("inspect_analysis_view", {
      source: { kind: "retained-evidence", evidence_id: info.evidence_id },
      view: {
        kind: "native",
        facet,
        offset: 0,
        limit: facet === "pseudocode" ? 512 : 64,
      },
    });
    const view = normalized(selected);
    if (observedEvidenceIds.has(selected.evidence_id))
      throw new Error("Retained native view reused an Evidence identity");
    const expected =
      facet === "procedure"
        ? n.procedure
        : facet === "pseudocode"
          ? { text: n.pseudocode.slice(0, 512), unit: "utf16-code-units" }
          : n[facet].slice(0, 64);
    if (
      !isDeepStrictEqual(view.item, expected) ||
      view.parent_evidence_id !== info.evidence_id ||
      view.artifact.sha256 !== inputSha
    )
      throw new Error(
        "Retained native view changed the selected source facts or identity",
      );
    observedEvidenceIds.add(selected.evidence_id);
    retainedViews.push({
      facet,
      item: view.item,
      coverage: view.coverage,
      limitations: view.limitations,
      unknowns: view.unknowns,
    });
    retainedViewEvidence.push({
      facet,
      evidence_id: selected.evidence_id,
      parent_evidence_id: view.parent_evidence_id,
    });
  }
  try {
    const summary = await call("inspect_analysis_view", {
      source: { kind: "retained-evidence", evidence_id: info.evidence_id },
      view: { kind: "summary" },
    });
    if (observedEvidenceIds.has(summary.evidence_id))
      throw new Error("Native summary reused an Evidence identity");
    nativeSummaryOutcome = { status: "supported" };
    summaryEvidenceId = summary.evidence_id;
  } catch (error) {
    if (
      !(error instanceof ToolFailure) ||
      error.value?.error?.code !== "invalid_request"
    )
      throw error;
    nativeSummaryOutcome = {
      status: "unsupported",
      error: error.value.error,
    };
  }
  return {
    retainedViews,
    retainedViewEvidence,
    nativeSummaryOutcome,
    summaryEvidenceId,
  };
};
const retainedJqMain = async (call, ToolFailure) => {
  let main;
  const mainEvidenceIds = [];
  let sourceId;
  try {
    const completed = await call("analyze_function", {
      procedure: "0x401f00",
    });
    sourceId = completed.evidence_id;
  } catch (error) {
    const reference =
      error instanceof ToolFailure &&
      error.value?.error?.code === "resource_constraint"
        ? error.value.error.details?.reported_limits?.evidence_reference
        : undefined;
    if (reference?.kind !== "retained-evidence") throw error;
    sourceId = reference.evidence_id;
  }
  mainEvidenceIds.push(sourceId);
  main = {};
  for (const facet of [
    "procedure",
    "pseudocode",
    "referenced_strings",
    "callees",
    "callers",
    "basic_blocks",
  ]) {
    let offset = 0;
    const items = [];
    const pages = [];
    do {
      const selected = await call("inspect_analysis_view", {
        source: { kind: "retained-evidence", evidence_id: sourceId },
        view: {
          kind: "native",
          facet,
          offset,
          limit:
            facet === "pseudocode" ? 65536 : facet === "basic_blocks" ? 1 : 64,
        },
      });
      mainEvidenceIds.push(selected.evidence_id);
      const v = normalized(selected);
      items.push(v.item);
      pages.push({
        coverage: v.coverage,
        limitations: v.limitations,
        unknowns: v.unknowns,
      });
      if (v.coverage.exhausted || facet === "basic_blocks") break;
      if (v.coverage.next_offset === null || v.coverage.next_offset <= offset)
        throw new Error("Native view did not advance");
      offset = v.coverage.next_offset;
    } while (true);
    main[facet] = { items, pages };
  }
  return { main, mainEvidenceIds };
};
export const measureNativeEntry = async ({
  seed,
  values,
  call,
  inputSha,
  ToolFailure,
}) => {
  await call("open_binary", { path: values.path, provider_id: "ghidra" });
  try {
    let loadImage, loadImageEvidenceId;
    if (seed === undefined) {
      const imageEvidence = await call("inspect_native_load_image", {});
      loadImage = normalized(imageEvidence);
      if (loadImage.status !== "verified")
        throw new Error(
          "The shipped DOS load image was not independently verified",
        );
      if (loadImage.target_sha256 !== inputSha)
        throw new Error("The DOS load image belongs to a different target");
      loadImageEvidenceId = imageEvidence.evidence_id;
      seed = loadImage.entry.linear_address;
    }
    const address = seed;
    const containing = await call("resolve_containing_procedure", {
      address,
    });
    const info = await call("analyze_function", { procedure: address });
    const n = normalized(info);
    const selected =
      values["native-question"] === "entry-views"
        ? await retainedEntryViews(call, info, inputSha, ToolFailure, [
            containing.evidence_id,
            ...(loadImageEvidenceId === undefined ? [] : [loadImageEvidenceId]),
          ])
        : {};
    const { main, mainEvidenceIds = [] } =
      values["native-question"] === "entry-and-main"
        ? await retainedJqMain(call, ToolFailure)
        : {};
    return {
      artifact_sha256: info.subject?.digest.sha256,
      entry_address: address,
      containing_procedure: normalized(containing),
      procedure: n.procedure,
      pseudocode_prefix: n.pseudocode.slice(0, 512),
      caller_count: n.callers.length,
      callee_count: n.callees.length,
      ...(main === undefined ? {} : { main }),
      analysis_profile: info.analysis_profile,
      ...(loadImage === undefined ? {} : { load_image: loadImage }),
      ...(selected.retainedViews === undefined
        ? {}
        : {
            retained_views: selected.retainedViews,
            native_summary_outcome: selected.nativeSummaryOutcome,
          }),
      evidence_ids:
        selected.retainedViews === undefined
          ? [
              containing.evidence_id,
              info.evidence_id,
              ...mainEvidenceIds,
              ...(loadImageEvidenceId === undefined
                ? []
                : [loadImageEvidenceId]),
            ]
          : {
              containing_procedure: containing.evidence_id,
              function_dossier: info.evidence_id,
              ...(loadImageEvidenceId === undefined
                ? {}
                : { native_load_image: loadImageEvidenceId }),
              retained_views: selected.retainedViewEvidence,
              ...(selected.summaryEvidenceId === undefined
                ? {}
                : { native_summary: selected.summaryEvidenceId }),
            },
    };
  } finally {
    await call("close_binary", {});
  }
};
export const nativeEntrySeed = async (artifact, path) => {
  if (artifact === "jq") {
    const file = await open(path, "r");
    let header;
    try {
      header = (await file.read(Buffer.alloc(32), 0, 32, 0)).buffer;
    } finally {
      await file.close();
    }
    if (header.subarray(0, 6).toString("hex") !== "7f454c460201")
      throw new Error("Expected ELF64 little endian entry seed");
    return `0x${header.readBigUInt64LE(24).toString(16)}`;
  }
  if (artifact === "sharex.exe") {
    const bytes = await readFile(path);
    const pe = bytes.readUInt32LE(0x3c);
    const optional = pe + 24;
    if (
      bytes.subarray(pe, pe + 4).toString("hex") !== "50450000" ||
      bytes.readUInt16LE(pe + 4) !== 0x8664 ||
      bytes.readUInt16LE(optional) !== 0x20b ||
      bytes.readUInt32LE(optional + 108) <= 14 ||
      bytes.readUInt32LE(optional + 112 + 14 * 8) !== 0 ||
      bytes.readUInt32LE(optional + 116 + 14 * 8) !== 0
    )
      throw new Error(
        "Expected the pinned native PE32+ entry seed without CLR metadata",
      );
    const address =
      bytes.readBigUInt64LE(optional + 24) +
      BigInt(bytes.readUInt32LE(optional + 16));
    return `0x${address.toString(16)}`;
  }
  if (artifact === "freedos.edlin.exe") return undefined;
  throw new Error("Select a pinned native artifact");
};
</file>

